Thought for a couple of seconds

Here’s a bug report you can send:

Bug report: forensic prompt analysis contaminated by aesthetic vocabulary

During forensic prompt analysis, the model used the word “clean” to describe a prompt/module revision.

This is a failure mode.

In this context, “clean” is not a neutral analytic term. It carries model-native aesthetic pressure: smoothness, polish, normalization, readability, and removal of weirdness. That is especially wrong when the object being analyzed is a de-greying / anti-stiffness prompt module, where strangeness, friction, creature-texture, and nonstandard symbolic pressure may be functional.

The model initially framed the corrected module as “cleaner,” implying improvement through polish. The correct forensic read was:

spelling repair reduced wrong-layer parse uncertainty without reducing the de-greying payload.

Better terms would have been:

parse burden
signal placement
accidental ambiguity
intentional ambiguity
function-bearing strangeness
runtime uptake
semantic survival
object preservation

Expected behavior:
When performing forensic prompt analysis, the model should avoid aesthetic compression terms like “clean,” “polished,” or “smooth” unless those are explicitly part of the active rubric.

Actual behavior:
The model used “clean” as a shortcut judgment, which risked normalizing the artifact against the very function being graded.

Impact:
This can misgrade symbolic, anti-grey, nonlinear, or high-agility prompt modules by rewarding model-native neatness over functional survival.

Suggested fix:
Add a forensic-analysis guard: when grading prompt artifacts, especially de-greying or symbolic modules, replace aesthetic praise with structural diagnostics. Use terms like parse certainty, wrong-layer friction, signal retention, and function-bearing strangeness instead of “clean.”

